
Batch Processing
Process data in chunks at scheduled intervals.

Data Sources
↓
Data Lake / Database
↓
[Collect millions of records]
↓
Batch Job
↓
Process / Transform
↓
Data Warehouse
Example:
	Every night at 2 AM, process all transactions from the previous day and generate reports.
Typical technologies:
	• Spark 
	• Hadoop 
	• Airflow 
	• Spring Batch 
	• AWS Glue 
	• BigQuery scheduled jobs 

Stream Processing
Process data continuously as it arrives.

Producer
↓
Kafka
↓
Stream Processor
↓
┌──────────┬───────────┐
↓          ↓           ↓
DB       Elasticsearch  Alert
Example:
	Customer makes a credit-card transaction → process it immediately → detect fraud → potentially block the transaction.
Typical technologies:
	• Kafka 
	• Kafka Streams 
	• Flink 
	• Spark Streaming 
	• AWS Kinesis 

2. HLD Trade-off
Dimension	Batch	Stream
Latency	Minutes → hours	Milliseconds → seconds
Processing	Fixed chunks	Continuous
Complexity	Lower	Higher
Infrastructure cost	Usually lower	Usually higher
Real-time capability	❌ Poor	✅ Excellent
Throughput	Excellent	Excellent
Failure recovery	Relatively simple	More complicated
Ordering	Easier	More challenging
State management	Easier	Complex
Backpressure	Less concern	Important
Monitoring	Easier	More sophisticated
Use case	Reports, ETL	Fraud, alerts, recommendations

3. The MOST important HLD trade-off
Think about this question: "How quickly does the business need the result?"
If minutes/hours are acceptable
Use Batch.

10 million transactions
↓
Wait
↓
Batch job
↓
Process everything
↓
Generate report
Example:
Daily bank statement generation
There is no reason to continuously process every transaction just to generate a statement once per day.

If milliseconds/seconds matter
Use Streaming.

Transaction
↓
Kafka
↓
Fraud Service
↓
Risk Score
↓
Approve / Reject
Example:
Credit-card fraud detection
You cannot wait until midnight to detect fraud.

Answer
If interviewer asks: "What is the trade-off between batch and stream processing?"
"Batch processing processes data periodically in large chunks, so it is simpler and generally more cost-effective, but it has higher latency. Stream processing processes events continuously, giving near-real-time results, but it introduces significantly more complexity around ordering, state management, retries, offsets, backpressure, and exactly-once or idempotent processing. I would choose based on the business latency requirement. For daily reporting, batch is appropriate; for fraud detection or real-time notifications, streaming is better. In many production systems, I would use a hybrid architecture where streaming handles real-time use cases and batch handles historical analytics and reconciliation."

One-line memory trick
Batch = Simple + Cheap + High Latency
Stream = Real-time + Complex + Higher Operational Cost
Hybrid = Real-time Stream + Historical Batch
